          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does adherence mean in the context of diabetes treatment, and why does it matter?",
          "answer": "In diabetes care, **adherence** means how closely a person follows the agreed treatment plan, such as taking glucose-lowering medication as prescribed and keeping up with monitoring and follow-up. The guidance in *Sticking with a diabetes regimen: monitoring, medication and review* says diabetes is largely self-managed. Most decisions that affect the outcome are made by the person between appointments.\n\nThe same guidance says that taking medication as prescribed is often imperfect in practice. It names common reasons: cost, complexity, side effects, low health literacy, depression, and simply forgetting. It notes that each of these has a different solution, so a missed dose is better explored than judged as a personal failing.\n\nThe guidance also describes structured diabetes self-management education as a core part of care. It says this education improves glucose control and quality of life, and that it is often underused.\n\nThe retrieved material does not explain in detail how missed doses change blood sugar over time, so I can't say more than that from these sources. If you are wondering about your own medication or routine, a clinician or diabetes educator is the right person to ask. Do not change any medication based on this information.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
